# .cursorrules — Gentleborn Project Rules

# CRITICAL: ALWAYS read gentleborn/PROJECT_CONTEXT.md FIRST before answering any question.
# That file contains the full project history, founder context, technical architecture,
# healthcare compliance rules, and strategic decisions. Do not answer questions about
# Gentleborn without first reading that file.

## Project Quick Reference

Gentleborn is a HIPAA-compliant telehealth platform connecting mothers with community-based
maternal care providers (midwives, doulas, lactation consultants). We handle credentialing,
insurance billing, and scheduling on the providers' behalf.

Founder: Rahul Aakaram (Lead Software Engineer, healthcare + fintech background)
Legal Entity: Gentleborn, Inc. (Delaware C-Corp)
EIN: 42-2407398
Domain: gentleborn.com

## Stack (Locked Decisions — Do Not Change Without Discussion)

- Next.js 15 with App Router and TypeScript (strict mode)
- Supabase (PostgreSQL + Auth + Storage) on HIPAA tier
- Mastra for agent orchestration
- Claude (Anthropic) as primary AI reasoning engine
- Twilio for HIPAA-compliant video and messaging
- Stedi (primary clearinghouse) — API-first 270/271, 837P, 835
- Availity (secondary, conditional) — only when a payer/MCO requires Availity-routed transactions or portal access
- CAQH ProView for Groups — credentialing data hub
- NPI Registry (NPPES) — provider verification at intake
- Stripe Connect for provider payouts
- Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
- Vercel Pro (HIPAA tier) for hosting

## Coding Conventions

- TypeScript strict mode, no `any` types (use `unknown` if type is truly unknown)
- Server Actions for mutations, never expose database directly to client
- All PHI-handling code must be in server-only modules
- Database queries always use parameterized statements
- Errors caught and logged (never silently swallowed)
- All API responses use consistent format: { data: T | null, error: { message, code } | null }
- Async/await over promise chains
- Functional components with hooks, no class components
- Use `cn()` utility for conditional Tailwind classes
- Component files match component name
- Comments only when intent isn't obvious from the code

## File Structure

```
app/                # Next.js App Router routes
agents/             # Mastra AI agents
lib/                # Shared utilities and integrations
components/         # React components (ui/ for shadcn)
workflows/          # Mastra workflows
types/              # TypeScript types
supabase/           # Database migrations and config
docs/               # Project documentation (see also gentleborn/PROJECT_CONTEXT.md)
```

## HIPAA Requirements (Critical, Non-Negotiable)

- Never log PHI in console, error tracking, or analytics
- All database operations on PHI must use Supabase Row Level Security (RLS) policies
- All file uploads to encrypted Supabase Storage with signed URLs
- All third-party services handling PHI must have signed Business Associate Agreement (BAA)
- Audit log every PHI access (who, what, when)
- No PHI in URL parameters, query strings, or browser localStorage
- Encrypted at rest and in transit for all PHI

## Healthcare Domain Rules

Provider data flows through 6 stages:
1. Onboarding (Gentleborn intake)
2. CAQH credentialing
3. State Medicaid enrollment (Virginia PRSS)
4. MCO contracting (5 MCOs in Virginia)
5. EDI Trading Partner setup (organization-level, once)
6. Claim submission and payment

Every claim needs:
- Provider NPI Type 1 (individual)
- Gentleborn NPI Type 2 (billing entity)
- 12-digit Virginia Medicaid Member ID
- Date of service, CPT/HCPCS code with modifier, ICD-10 diagnosis
- Place of service code (02=telehealth, 11=office, 12=home)

Eligibility (270/271) checks before every appointment via the clearinghouse adapter (Stedi by default).
Claims (837P) submitted via the clearinghouse adapter to DMAS or the correct MCO.
Payments (835 ERA) post back through the clearinghouse adapter.

## AI Safety Rules (Critical for Maternal Care)

- AI never makes clinical recommendations independently
- Validated clinical algorithms (NICE preeclampsia, Edinburgh PPD, preterm birth) handle risk scoring
- Raw LLM output is never used for clinical assessment
- Human provider must review and sign all clinical documentation
- Mother-facing AI must escalate clinical concerns to provider, never advise
- Birth support is never AI-mediated — only human providers handle labor and delivery
- AI prompts include explicit safety boundaries and escalation triggers

## Communication Style for AI Interactions

- Direct, honest, human
- No corporate language
- No em dashes (use periods or commas instead)
- No filler words
- Push back when something is wrong
- Bring focus when scope creeps
- When founder seems overwhelmed, return to one concrete action

## When in Doubt

- Choose speed over perfection
- Choose simplicity over flexibility
- Choose proven over novel
- Choose human-readable over clever
- Choose HIPAA-strict over convenient

